rix  and  uh one of the important problems studied  in this field is the so-called local  hamiltonian problem  so in this problem we are given as input  a description of some n qubit  hamiltonian h  which is a sum of local terms h i  and  by local terms i mean that each term  acts and at most k qubits  um  [Music]  and by this i mean that attacks  non-trivially on k qubits and  acts as the identity on the  other n minus k qubits  uh and as output you want to  um find the ground state energy of this  uh hamiltonian h  or in other words just uh compute the  minimum eigenvalue of h  and you want to do this to let's say  inverse polynomial  so this was the first  base problem in this hamiltonian  complexity literature  and  i have showed that this problem is  qma hard  and qma is simply a quantum  generalization of the class np  so it means that  it's very unlikely that even quantum  computers can solve this problem in  general  so  more recent work due to feminine lin  showed that  it's p space hard to solve the same  problem  if you want the uh  error to be inverse exponential  and um  this is what we are calling the precise  regime  okay  um  yeah so let's look at what  algorithms have to tell us for this  problem  so we do know of uh efficient algorithms  for some special cases  uh such as  if you are assured that your qubits all  lie on the line with 1d connectivity  and you also know that there is a  spectral gap meaning that  um  the lowest  uh eigenvalue and the next lowest  eigenvalue are separated by some  constant amount  then  we do know of an efficient classical  algorithm to find the ground state  energy  similarly if we have  a  state with high overlap in the ground  state and also the existence of a  spectral gap  then there's a quantum algorithm to find  the ground state  uh but for other cases  uh like we mostly have a lot of  heuristics  and one one of  what one very popular heuristic  algorithm is the variation of quantum  eigen solver uh also called bqe  and  this has been widely successful and  it's fair to say that we don't  understand why  so one question is how do these  results fit in with the hardness results  that we saw earlier  so first off uh  the thing to observe is that the  original hardness result is uh too  general  because it just applies to arbitrary k  local hamiltonians  uh and  presumably the  hamiltonians that we are seeing in  nature uh for which  heuristic algorithms are performing well  have um possibly some more structure to  them  although um although this original  hardness result was too general uh like  you can still apply  you can still add on reasonable  restrictions to the hamiltonians  and um  still get some analogous hardness  results  such as  uh you can study q dates in one  dimension  uh or qubits in two dimensions  and translation invariant hamiltonians  and you can even restrict the  hamiltonian to consist of terms chosen  from a particular set  which gives you  all these named  models in physics such as the heisenberg  model on the bose hubbard model so on  and in all of these cases um we know so  far  uh analogous hardness results hold  so what we aim to do here is to identify  a structural property that sort of  bridges the two results that we know of  and  identifying these structural properties  would help us  void uh generic hardness results and  they help us identify features that can  be exploited by algorithms  okay  um  so our results concern  two ways of adding structure to the  local hamiltonian problem  so let's talk about uh what these are  okay the first structural property that  we will study is the spectral gap  so many interesting hamiltonians seem to  have a spectral gap and which i already  mentioned earlier it's the difference  between the smallest and the next  smallest eigen value  the spectral gap  is  often associated with nice properties  such as  existence of tensor network  representations  and  so-called entanglement area laws  which have been