ide  of the inverse exponential precision  setting  the second structural property we'll be  studying  is that of um  an existence of  classical descriptions of ground states  in physics we often want these non  trivial or numbing size descriptions of  ground states from which we can  efficiently compute  certain properties such as say the  energy or some local observable  and  it's not  uh obvious that  these descriptions exist  but for some cases they do  and some examples are say tensor network  descriptions from which you can compute  observables  another example  is say that of  an explicit description of a polynomial  sized quantum circuit that would prepare  the ground state uh now this  may not be  uh useful classically but  if you have a quantum computer then you  can  you know go ahead and prepare the ground  state and study its properties  so do such descriptions always exist for  local hamiltonians it's not clear a  priority  um  and yeah in if this if the answer turns  out to be yes then  that will imply that two  complexity classes are equal to one  another  so qma is just the analog of  uh  it's just a quantum analog of np  where merlin is a quantum witness and  arthur has a quantum computer  whereas qcma is another quantum  generalization of np  where the witness is classical  but the verifier is  still has a quantum computer  okay so  the difference between these two classes  qma and qcma  is essentially  all about whether  ground states of local hamiltonians  have efficient classical weaknesses for  them  and  given that this  question of qma versus qcma is so  interesting  no doubt there has been work uh done on  it before  so we know that there's some oracle  evidence that the two classes are  different  um  so yeah this is asking the question of  how rich is this set of states with  polynomial circuit complexity  and  for example whether it encompasses uh  the set of ground states of local  hamiltonians  and  there's not really a nice analog of 