 a spectral gap uh  coincide  and  in fact we have proved um  a version of this for like  um  for the case where you consider  inverse exponential position so that  adds this modifier precise to the two  clusters  okay uh so i'll just broadly describe um  our proof ideas  uh so for the pp up around in both cases  uh what we do is prepare a thermal state  at low enough temperature  and  a thermal state is just one given by the  gibbs distribution  so if you have  a constant spectral gap or even an  inverse polynomial spectral gap  this would ensure that  the thermal state has pretty good  overlap with the ground state  and  because  of this thermal state representation  you can write out the energy  in terms of a large sum  which can be computed in pp  uh as for the lower bound we modify this  uh construction of analog at all  uh which proved that  adiabatic quantum computing is universal  so we actually use another tool um  called the small penalty clock  construction  and  this is a technique  that we have come up with which  allows one to use perturbation theory  and  helps keep track of eigen values  and  helps you lower bound the spectral gap  of the  resulting hamiltonian that you get after  the reduction  so in other words it can be viewed as  spectral gap preserving production  okay  so to conclude um  here's a summary of what we know about  the local hamiltonian problem  when considering inverse exponential  additive error  in the case where the spectral gap  was only known to be  larger than or equal to b equal to zero  or in other words when there was no  uh when there was only a vacuous promise  on the spectral graph  um  the problem is p space complete when you  have a quantum witness  and np to the bp complete when you have  classical witness  and the addition of a spectral gap of  inverse polynomial  immediately reduces the complexity of  both cases to  pp  but in the classical case  the reduction of complexity is  you know not  uh more stringent in th